<body>
\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>A <strong>DHCP Relay</strong> agent resolves a fundamental networking limitation: initial <a href="what-is-dhcp.html">DHCP</a> Discover messages are sent as Layer 2 broadcasts, and routers generally do not forward Layer 2 broadcasts between different subnets.</p>
            
            <h3>The Problem</h3>
            <p>Imagine a network with a centralized DHCP server:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Client VLAN:</strong> 192.168.20.0/24</li>
                <li><strong>DHCP Server:</strong> 192.168.100.10</li>
            </ul>
            <p>When a client boots on VLAN 20, it shouts a broadcast packet looking for an IP address. The router blocking broadcasts means the packet never reaches the DHCP server at 192.168.100.10.</p>
            
            <h3>The Relay Flow</h3>
            <p>By configuring a DHCP Relay on the Layer 3 gateway (the router interface for VLAN 20), the flow changes:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>The Client sends a DHCP broadcast.</li>
                <li>The Layer 3 gateway intercepts the broadcast.</li>
                <li>The gateway encapsulates it into a unicast packet and <em>relays</em> it directly to the DHCP Server IP. It populates a specific field called the <strong>GIADDR</strong> (Gateway IP Address) with its own interface IP (e.g., 192.168.20.1).</li>
                <li>The DHCP server receives the unicast packet. By looking at the GIADDR field, the server knows the client is on the 192.168.20.0/24 subnet and selects an IP from that specific scope.</li>
                <li>The server replies to the relay, which forwards it back to the client.</li>
            </ol>
            
            <h3>Terminology and Troubleshooting</h3>
            <p><strong>"ip helper-address"</strong> is vendor-specific terminology commonly associated with Cisco-style configuration to enable DHCP relay functionality. It is not a universal command.</p>
            <p>If utilizing a relay and clients aren't getting IPs, check your <a href="dhcp-troubleshooting-guide.html">DHCP troubleshooting</a> basics:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Is the relay configured on the correct VLAN interface?</li>
                <li>Does the gateway have routing reachability to the server?</li>
                <li>Are firewalls permitting UDP ports 67 and 68?</li>
                <li>Does the server have a scope explicitly configured for the relay's GIADDR subnet?</li>
            </ul>
        
        </div>
        
